Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/mediacodecdec: export the decoder's static HDR metadata on every frame

On an NVIDIA SHIELD (Plezy #2955), an HDR10 file whose mastering display
and content light metadata sit only in its SEI showed mpv's
`video-params` as max-luma 10000, min-luma 0, max-cll 0 and max-fall 0:
mpv's inferred PQ peak and nothing else. FFmpeg's own HEVC decoder (every
hwaccel, software decode) reads that SEI and attaches it to each frame;
the MediaCodec wrapper never sees the bitstream's SEI, and a frame it
wraps for a surface never passes through ff_get_buffer, so not even the
container's metadata (an MP4's mdcv and clli boxes) reached it. Only
Matroska's Colour element got through, because mpv's own demuxer reads it.

MediaCodec reports what it parsed in its output format, as the
"hdr-static-info" CTA-861.3 Static Metadata Descriptor; OMX.Nvidia.h265.decode
reports the stream's values there once its first frame is out, and all zero
before that and for ByteBuffer output. The output format parser now reads
that key into the context, holding the mastering display to the ranges
FFmpeg's decoders hold the same SEI to (h2645_sei.c) and taking zero light
levels as unknown. Both wrap paths attach it to the frame as mastering
display and content light side data the way FFmpeg's decoders do: over the
container's, unless the caller prefers the container's
(side_data_prefer_packet). A copied frame has the container's from
ff_get_buffer; a surface frame now gets whichever of the two wins, and
only that one is allocated.

Reading the key needed the JNI MediaFormat getBuffer to work at all: it
read the value through GetDirectBufferAddress, but MediaFormat's buffers
are heap buffers (android_media_Utils wraps a byte array), so it handed
back NULL with a size of (size_t)-1 and success. It now copies the
buffer's remaining bytes through ByteBuffer.duplicate() and get(byte[]),
and checks for a pending exception before it trusts containsKey's answer;
the NDK getBuffer, which lent the format's own storage, returns a copy
too, so both return memory the caller frees. Nothing called getBuffer
before.

diff --git a/libavcodec/mediacodec_wrapper.c b/libavcodec/mediacodec_wrapper.c
index 6cae0eb..f03e3fc 100644
--- a/libavcodec/mediacodec_wrapper.c
+++ b/libavcodec/mediacodec_wrapper.c
@@ -113,6 +113,13 @@ struct JNIAMediaFormatFields {
 
     jmethodID to_string_id;
 
+    // MediaFormat's byte buffers are heap buffers (android_media_Utils wraps a
+    // byte array), which JNI's direct-buffer access cannot read.
+    jclass bytebuffer_class;
+    jmethodID bytebuffer_duplicate_id;
+    jmethodID bytebuffer_remaining_id;
+    jmethodID bytebuffer_get_id;
+
 };
 
 #define OFFSET(x) offsetof(struct JNIAMediaFormatFields, x)
@@ -137,6 +144,11 @@ static const struct FFJniField jni_amediaformat_mapping[] = {
 
         { "android/media/MediaFormat", "toString", "()Ljava/lang/String;", FF_JNI_METHOD, OFFSET(to_string_id), 1 },
 
+    { "java/nio/ByteBuffer", NULL, NULL, FF_JNI_CLASS, OFFSET(bytebuffer_class), 1 },
+        { "java/nio/ByteBuffer", "duplicate", "()Ljava/nio/ByteBuffer;", FF_JNI_METHOD, OFFSET(bytebuffer_duplicate_id), 1 },
+        { "java/nio/ByteBuffer", "remaining", "()I", FF_JNI_METHOD, OFFSET(bytebuffer_remaining_id), 1 },
+        { "java/nio/ByteBuffer", "get", "([B)Ljava/nio/ByteBuffer;", FF_JNI_METHOD, OFFSET(bytebuffer_get_id), 1 },
+
     { NULL }
 };
 #undef OFFSET
@@ -973,53 +985,71 @@ fail:
 
